k2day":"nearZero","prob7day":"0%","risk7day":"nearZero","idp_source":"gtwo_kml_202611212309","idp_filedate":1795302555000,"nhcTitle":"Offshore of the Southeast U.S. Coast","discussion":"A non-tropical gale-force low pressure area centered a few hundred  miles southeast of the North Carolina coast is producing  disorganized showers and thunderstorms primarily along its  associated stationary front. This low is forecast to move northward  into unfavorable environmental conditions, and development into a  tropical or subtropical system is no longer expected.  1. Regardless of the system's status, this large weather system is  still expected to produce gusty winds, dangerous surf, rip currents,  and coastal flooding along portions of the southeast and  mid-Atlantic U.S. coasts into early next week. In addition, heavy  rainfall and the threat of flash flooding will move northward from  coastal portions of the Carolinas to the mid-Atlantic over the next  day or so. Interests along the U.S. East Coast should continue to  monitor the progress of this system. Additional information on this  system, including Gale Warnings, can be found in High Seas Forecasts  issued by the National Weather Service, as well as products issued  by your local National Weather Service forecast office. \n Formation chance through 48 hours...low...near 0 percent. \n Formation chance through 7 days...low...near 0 percent.","disturbance":1}}]}